  <p><em>This article was discussed <a href=3D"https://old.reddit.com/r/C_P=
rogramming/comments/111238u/lets_implement_buffered_formatted_output/">on r=
eddit</a>.</em></p>

<p>When <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/02/11/">not using the =
C standard library</a>, how does one deal with
formatted output? Re-implementing the entirety of <code class=3D"language-p=
laintext highlighter-rouge">printf</code> from scratch
seems like a lot of work, and indeed it would be. Fortunately it=E2=80=99s =
rarely
necessary. With the right mindset, and considering your program=E2=80=99s <=
em>actual</em>
formatting needs, it=E2=80=99s not as difficult as it might appear. Since i=
t goes
hand-in-hand with buffering, I=E2=80=99ll cover both topics at once, includ=
ing
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">sprintf</code>-like ca=
pabilities, which is where we=E2=80=99ll start.</p>

<!--more-->

<h3 id=3D"the-print-is-append-mindset">The print-is-append mindset</h3>

<p>Buffering amortizes the costs of write (and read) system calls. Many sma=
ll
writes are queued via the buffer into a few large writes. This isn=E2=80=99=
t just
an implementation detail. It=E2=80=99s key in the mindset to tackle formatt=
ed
output: <strong>Printing is appending.</strong></p>

<p>The mindset includes the reverse: <em>Appending is like printing</em>. C=
onsider
this next time you reach for <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-=
rouge">strcat</code> or similar. Is this the appropriate
destination for this data, or am I just going to print it =E2=80=94 i.e. ap=
pend it
to another, different buffer =E2=80=94 afterward?</p>

<p>This concept may sound obvious, but consider that there are major, popul=
ar
programming paradigms where the norm is otherwise. I=E2=80=99ll pick on Pyt=
hon to
illustrate, but it=E2=80=99s not alone.</p>

<div class=3D"language-py highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre =
class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"k">print</span><span class=3D"p">(=
</span><span class=3D"sa">f</span><span class=3D"s">"found </span><span cla=
ss=3D"si">{</span><span class=3D"n">count</span><span class=3D"si">}</span>=
<span class=3D"s"> items"</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>This line of code allocates a buffer; formats the value of the variable
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">count</code> into it; =
allocates a second buffer; copies into it the prefix
(<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">"found "</code>), the=
 first buffer, and the suffix (<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighte=
r-rouge">" items"</code>); copies the
contents of this second buffer into the standard output buffer; then
discards the two temporary buffers. To see for yourself, use the <a href=3D=
"https://docs.python.org/3/library/dis.html">CPython
bytecode disassembler</a> on it. (It <em>is</em> pretty neat that string
formatting is partially implemented in the compiler and partially parsed
at compile time.)</p>

<p>With the print-is-append mindset, you know it=E2=80=99s ultimately being=
 copied
into the standard output buffer, and that you can skip the intermediate
appending and copying. Avoiding that pessimization isn=E2=80=99t just about=
 the
computer=E2=80=99s time, it=E2=80=99s even more about saving your own time =
implementing
formatted output.</p>

<p>In C that line looks like:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">printf</span><span class=3D"p">(=
</span><span class=3D"s">"found %d items</span><span class=3D"se">\n</span>=
<span class=3D"s">"</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">coun=
t</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The format string is a domain-specific language (DSL) that is (usually)
parsed and evaluated at run time. In essence it=E2=80=99s a little program =
that
says:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Append <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">"found "<=
/code> to the output buffer</li>
  <li>Format the given integer into the output buffer</li>
  <li>Append <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">" items\n=
"</code> to the output buffer</li>
</ol>

<p>For <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">sprintf</code> =
the output buffer is caller-supplied instead of a buffered
stream.</p>

<p>In this implementation we=E2=80=99re doing to skip the DSL and express s=
uch
=E2=80=9Cformat programs=E2=80=9D in C itself. It=E2=80=99s more verbose at=
 the call site, but it
simplifies the implementation. As a bonus, it=E2=80=99s also faster since t=
he
format program is itself compiled by the C compiler. In your own formatted
output implementation you could write a <code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">printf</code> that, following the
format string, calls the append primitives we=E2=80=99ll build below.</p>

<h3 id=3D"buffer-implementation">Buffer implementation</h3>

<p>Let=E2=80=99s begin by defining an output buffer. An output buffer track=
s the
total capacity and how much has been written. I=E2=80=99ll include a sticky=
 error
flag to simplify error checks. For a first pass we=E2=80=99ll start with a
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">sprintf</code> rather =
than full-blown <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">printf=
</code> because there=E2=80=99s nowhere yet for
the data to go.</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"cp">#define MEMBUF(buf, cap) {buf, =
