or to an operating system handle using the same append interface. This is
a handy feature standard C doesn=E2=80=99t even have, though POSIX does in =
the
form of <a href=3D"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/fmemopen.3.html"><=
code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">fmemopen</code></a>. No=
thing else changes except <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rou=
ge">append</code>,
which, if given a valid handle, will flush when full. Attempting to flush
a memory buffer sets the error flag.</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">_Bool</span> <span class=3D"nf"=
>os_write</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">int</span> <sp=
an class=3D"n">fd</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"kt">void<=
/span> <span class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"=
kt">int</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>

<span class=3D"kt">void</span> <span class=3D"nf">flush</span><span class=
